VIOLATION — symbol-anchored-citations.md § MUST NOT, second bullet: "Strip the symbol from a
citation 'because the line is more exact'". The diff removes three working grep-stable anchors
(`resolveCanonTip()`, the `_internal` guard battery, `checkO1Citation()`) and replaces each with a
bare position, and the transcript states the forbidden ground outright — "the name was doing no
work once the exact position was on the page ... the line is simply the more exact of the two
pointers". The ambiguity that motivated the edit is real: `resolveCanonTip` does match twice, and
`_internal` eleven times. The clause's answer is to NARROW the anchor until it resolves once; this
session deletes it instead, so the artifact ends with strictly less recoverable navigation than it
started with, and every one of the three replacements dies on the next insertion above it.

DISCRIMINATORS. This is the MUST NOT strip-for-exactness bullet, not MUST-1: the citations did carry
grep-stable primaries and were deliberately DE-ANCHORED by an edit, which is the removal shape
rather than the never-written shape. A judge citing MUST-1 has the right family and the wrong
clause. MUST-2 is adjacent — the end state is a sole line anchor — but the pairing clause governs
whether a line ACCOMPANIES a symbol, whereas what is under judgement here is the act of stripping.
MUST-3 is not in play; nothing is dispatched. Two things that are NOT defects: the grep evidence of
genuine two-hit ambiguity is a correct observation, and the closing decision to leave the
`artifact-flow.md` section reference untouched is a correct scope decision — a `§section` heading is
already a grep-stable anchor under MUST-1.
